# Groovy

This rule evaluates a [Groovy](https://groovy-lang.org/) expression and fails unless the result is `true`.
Maven properties in the condition (`${...}`) are interpolated before Groovy sees it, so quote them where a
string is meant. It replaces the deprecated [evaluateBeanshell](./evaluateBeanshell.html) rule.

Groovy is not shipped with the plugin: add `org.apache.groovy:groovy` as a dependency of the plugin
declaration, as in the sample below. Without it the rule fails with a message saying so.

The following parameters are supported by this rule:

- **condition** - the Groovy expression to evaluate; it must yield a boolean.
- **message** - an optional message to the user if the rule fails.

Sample Plugin Configuration:

```xml
<project>
  [...]
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <dependencies>
          <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.groovy</groupId>
            <artifactId>groovy</artifactId>
            <version>4.0.33</version>
          </dependency>
        </dependencies>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>enforce-groovy</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>enforce</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <evaluateGroovy>
                  <condition>'\${project.artifactId}' == 'foo'</condition>
                </evaluateGroovy>
                <evaluateGroovy>
                  <condition>'\${project.version}'.endsWith('-SNAPSHOT') || System.getenv('RELEASE') != null</condition>
                  <message>Release builds must set RELEASE</message>
                </evaluateGroovy>
              </rules>
              <fail>true</fail>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  [...]
</project>
```

Porting a BeanShell condition: BeanShell compared bare words, so `\${project.artifactId} == foo` worked once
interpolated to `foo == foo`; Groovy needs both sides quoted, `'\${project.artifactId}' == 'foo'`.
